 still very bad  indeed for the UK and for many other  countries but it is not quite the same  picture as you might have seen from  those overall numbers the UK there below  Italy below Spain and actually the  country with the most severe covered 19  outbreak as in population adjusted terms  this is Belgium look at bottom over  there 694 deaths per million of the  population and what this underlines is  that there are so many different ways of  measuring this I'm not saying anyone is  the definitive but just bear that in  mind when you hear these scary numbers  I'll give you  there's three three key measures we're  gonna bear in mind that the first of  them you'll have heard a lot and this is  the one we're talking about at the  moment which is the numbers that the  government give us every day on how many  people have died of covet 19 you can see  a few a week and a half ago it was up to  twenty-two thousand according to what we  got from the government each day but  that's not everything we also get more  coherent more comprehensive numbers from  the ons which include everyone who's  died of covert 19 or at least who has  Coppa 19 on their death certificate and  their numbers show actually at that  point it was higher so not twenty two  thousand but twenty-six thousand but  according to the chief scientific  adviser there is a different way  different kind of number we should be  looking at which is not just covert  nineteen diagnose deaths but all excess  deaths all excess deaths which are  considerably higher and I'm going to  show you why that matters if we look at  what's happened in the care home sector  this line here shows you how many people  died each week of the year just over the  course of the last few years so the  average number of people who died in in  care homes over the year and what we do  if we look at just where we are this  years so far you can see excess deaths  so the number of people who have died  above that line have risen to 16,000 in  